      <section class="faith-mf-panel" id="story" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mf-tab-story" data-mf-panel>
        <header class="faith-mf-head">
          <h2 class="faith-mf-title">The Story of <em>The Faith Received</em></h2>
          <p class="faith-mf-by">by Ian Harber</p>
          <p class="faith-mf-role">Co-Principal Architect of <em>The Faith Received</em><br />Director of Communications and Marketing of Mere Orthodoxy</p>
        </header>
        <p>There have been a number of historic Christian works that have been hugely helpful and influential in my life, especially as I came out of a prolonged season of doubt and deconstruction. Obviously the <a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=apostles-creed">Apostles</a> and <a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=nicene-creed">Nicene</a> creeds are part of that. But so are works like <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=athanasius-of-alexandria">Athanasius</a>’s <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=pg-2880">On the Incarnation</a></em>, <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=irenaeus-of-lyon">Irenaeus</a>’s <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=pg-2847">Against Heresies</a></em>, <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=augustine-of-hippo">Augustine</a>’s <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=pld-2722">Confessions</a></em>, <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=Boethius">Boethius</a>’s <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=pld-3278">The Consolation of Philosophy</a></em>, <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=Thomas%20%C3%A0%20Kempis">Thomas à Kempis</a>’s <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=imitation-of-christ">The Imitation of Christ</a></em>, and selections from <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=john-calvin">Calvin</a>’s <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=calvin-institutes-of-the-christian-religion">Institutes</a></em>. Other works like <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=didache">The Didache</a></em>, <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=pg-30">The Letter to Diognetus</a></em>, <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=rc-061-heidelberg-catechism-1563">The Heidelberg Catechism</a></em>, and the <em><a href="/the-faith-received/read/?w=1928-bcp">Book of Common Prayer</a></em> have helped root me in something substantial when my faith was still looking for ground to stand on.</p>
        <p>After a while, I had the recurring wish that I could carry all of these works around with me in my pocket. They are the reference points in my mind for so many things and yet I could never pull them up when I wanted to actually reference them. Some of these works do exist elsewhere, but they weren’t easy to find, helpful to use, or beautiful to read.</p>
        <p>So I decided to do something about it. In 2023, I had the idea for <em>The Faith Received</em>. I bought the domain name and started putting some of these works on a simple website. I never told anyone about it. It was just for me with a glimmer of hope that one day it might be something for others too. But for three years, the site sat dormant and unused.</p>
        <p>Until April of 2026 when I began redesigning the <em>Mere Orthodoxy</em> website. It occurred to me that I could use all of the same tools I was using for <em>Mere Orthodoxy</em> to finally create <em>The Faith Received</em> and make it as good as I thought it deserved to be. I also realized that a project like this might fit perfectly with <em>Mere Orthodoxy’s</em> mission of bringing the best of the church’s thinking to bear on our present day. I brought the idea to Mark and Jake and they agreed that it fit. So we decided to start working on it after the new website launched.</p>
        <p>I had a basic prototype working when I got a call from one of <em>Mere Orthodoxy’s</em> contributors, Stiven Peter. Stiven told me that he had just spent a few months using artificial intelligence to translate hundreds of primary source works from church history into English for the very first time and wanted to know if <em>Mere Orthodoxy</em> wanted to publish them. I couldn’t believe what I was hearing. I told Stiven he had to see what I was working on. I showed him <em>The Faith Received</em> and we both realized these were the same project. We needed to add as many works from the history of the church to <em>The Faith Received</em> as possible.</p>
        <p>From then on, Stiven worked on translating thousands of works and thinking through the features that would be the most helpful for pastors, academics, and anyone who is trying to find their place in the faith. I worked on making <em>The Faith Received</em> as beautiful and usable of a platform as I possibly could, making it a place where people would actually want to dive into this library and have a great experience doing it.</p>
        <p>This is the website I wish I had when I was younger. When I had doubts and felt alone in my faith, I wish I could have found myself surrounded by the witness of the saints who had gone before me and passed down the faith to be received. Some of it was simply inaccessible or nearly unreadable. Some of it had never even been translated to our language before.</p>
        <p>All of that has changed.</p>
        <p><em>The Faith Received</em> is built to make these works of historic Christian theology publicly accessible, practically usable, and beautifully readable. It stands as the theological leg of <em>Mere Orthodoxy’s</em> stool and our hope is that it serves our generation and generations to come.</p>
