      <section class="faith-mf-panel" id="mere-orthodoxy" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mf-tab-mo" data-mf-panel>
        <header class="faith-mf-head">
          <h2 class="faith-mf-title">Mere Orthodoxy and <em>The Faith Received</em></h2>
          <p class="faith-mf-by">by Jake Meador</p>
          <p class="faith-mf-role">Editor-in-Chief of Mere Orthodoxy</p>
        </header>
        <p>Dear readers,</p>
        <p>We have some big news today. If you want to skip straight to it you can scroll down to the next header. But I think what follows below is helpful for understanding why we are launching a new initiative today called <em>The Faith Received</em>, so I’d encourage you to read first and then get to the launch announcement.</p>
        <h3>Counties, Frontiers, and Cities</h3>
        <p>When I was growing up in a fundamentalist church, we had a simple way of thinking about church life and Christian theology: In practice, we related to the faith the way a surveyor relates to the boundaries between counties. If you drive about 30 minutes from my home in Lincoln, you’ll pass a sign welcoming you to Saunders County. That’s how all our states are split up in the US, of course: The states are chopped up into small geographic regions called counties and at all times you’re either in one county or another. It is not possible to be a little bit in Lancaster County and a little bit in Saunders County. This is how we approached the Christian life at my church growing up.</p>
        <p>On every imaginable theological position we took as a church, that position simply was Christianity. Anything that wasn’t that position, was not. We were the Christian county, so to speak, and everything that disagreed with us was plainly from some other not-Christian county. So, for example, it was quite common to speak of both Calvinistic Christians and Roman Catholic Christians as non-Christians. Indeed, it sometimes veered toward the plainly absurd: A former seminary professor of our pastor used the phrase “half-born again” to describe professing Christians who did not believe in a young earth and seven 24-hour day creation account. Of course, given that it is not actually possible to be “half” born, this was just a particularly bizarre way of saying “not really Christian.”</p>
        <p>The trouble here is you can’t sustain this sort of thing with much honesty. You definitely can’t sustain it once you start reading historic Christian theology. But even just the Bible alone is enough to refute it. After all it was St Paul–nobody’s idea of a squish–who said that if you confess with your mouth Jesus as Lord and believe in your heart that God raised him from the dead, you will be saved. I remember the first time I read that verse after I started to question fundamentalism. It messed with my head. By that time I had known enough Christians from other churches to know that they all, even the Catholics!, professed with their mouths that Jesus was Lord and believed that God raised him from the dead. So what then? Were they Christians? Paul would say “yes,” seemingly. So why didn’t we? I didn’t have an answer to that.</p>
        <p>Eventually I had to leave that church and I found my way, for a short time, to broader evangelicalism. The sort of County Christianity I knew as a child was nowhere to be found here. But what I did find wasn’t especially helpful either: Instead of counties, we seemed to have frontiers: we had no history, no infrastructure, no boundaries, save those asserted by the individual leadership of individual churches.</p>
        <p>If County Christianity offered a certain degree of clarity, but at the cost of honesty and charity, then the Frontier Christianity offered open possibilities and energy, but at the expense of clarity and fidelity. It took only about 12 months for me, an intellectually engaged and curious young adult, to start asking questions that no one at the church seemed able to answer. So I left. The County Christianity couldn’t engage history or questions with any integrity. But the Frontier Christianity seemed unable to engage history or questions either, unless you approached them in the highly limited and quixotic way your particular congregation did—and even then it may not be sufficient.</p>
        <p>I needed something different.</p>
        <p>My guess is if you’re reading this you probably understand that struggle. You recognize that people and history and theology and church life are all far too complex for the trite answers of fundamentalism. But when you have gone in search of depth, clarity, and seriousness in broader evangelicalism you have too often found faddishness, triviality, and a very tribal mode of intellectual life that consists largely of amassing a small set of clearly labeled boxes sorted by worldview into which you can place the ideas you encounter as you go about your life.</p>
        <p>At Mere Orthodoxy we have always tried to offer something distinct from either the County or the Frontier. We have offered our readers something more like a city–the messy and complicated and beautiful thing that is Christian intellectual history down through the ages. There are buildings here of varying degrees of soundness and beauty and age. There are also real boundaries, but also a lot of gradation: after all, cities don’t really have boundaries in the way a county does, though they do have limits. We know Bloomington MN is not part of New York City, for example. But it is part of the Twin Cities, even if it is not named “St Paul” or “Minneapolis.” There are less explored parts of town too as well as neighborhoods ripe for development and new life. Much of our work could be thought of as trying to help Christians, particularly Protestant Christians, recognize that the church is a city, not a county or a frontier, that it is their city, and that within it there is both clarity and rigor as well as room to breathe and explore and enjoy the common life you share with your brothers and sisters in Christ.</p>
        <h3>Introducing <em>The Faith Received</em></h3>
        <p>While that has always been our heart at Mere Orthodoxy, our work has traditionally consisted of commentary around the news of the day, books old and new, and the many theological and cultural debates Christians have always had.</p>
        <p>That began to change this past August with the addition of “The Daily Liturgy” to our podcast network: Now in addition to commentary, we have an aid in piety and discipleship that can help you begin or end your day with prayer and scripture. But our plan was never to stop there. Rather, we want to build an online hub to aid churches, other Christian groups, and individual Christian believers in comprehensive Christian formation–social, spiritual, and intellectual. Today is the next step in that work.</p>
        <p><em>The Faith Received</em> is a collection of historic creeds, confessions, catechisms, theological works, and biblical commentaries now freely available to all Mere Orthodoxy readers. We have built TFR to be publicly available, practically usable, and beautifully readable.</p>
        <p>You can read <a href="#what" data-mf-tab-link>the introductory essay by TFR principal architects Stiven Peter and Ian Harber</a> to learn more about the project itself. For my purposes here, I want to briefly come back to that city I mentioned above. I think the city has seen better days in many ways. Certainly there are lots of beautiful old buildings now largely forgotten or abandoned. TFR is something like a thorough account of the city’s history: historic photos, blueprints, phonebooks, and all the rest. If you want to work for renewal, to remember the glories that have existed in the past and could exist again… well, this is for you. We want <em>The Faith Received</em> to help you see more clearly what the city has been, and what your inheritance as a member of that city is.</p>
        <p>We cannot simply repristinate the city that was, of course. The reality of sin as well as our own finitude means that will never happen. But we can receive our inheritance with gratitude and seek to live into it and improve upon it. That is the goal of this project. Dive into the texts. Ask your questions. Learn. Think. Probe. And do so with a sense of freedom and joy.</p>
        <p>You don’t need to police every boundary, but you also don’t need to ignore history altogether. History certainly isn’t bunk, but neither is it a straitjacket. You can receive it, learn from it, and commend it to others. Be “untroubled in your seeking” as Joe Pug once put it in a favorite song of mine. And, with the aid of your fathers and mothers in the faith, take your learning into the world and better radiate the love of God to your neighbor.</p>
        <h3>Does this contradict your other policies regarding generative artificial intelligence?</h3>
        <p>I imagine some of you will have that question, especially given that we only just published a writer’s covenant last week that placed some significant limits on how we expect our writers to use generative AI. I want to address that here, briefly, though we’ll have more on this in the months to come. There are a few points that I want to make regarding our use of AI to translate many of these texts as well as the chat bot you’ll find inside TFR.</p>
        <p>First, let’s talk about the research tools in <em>The Faith Received</em>. As we noted in our writer covenant last week, we have never had a policy of banning the use of AI for research purposes. Indeed, there is no point in doing so since the only way to fully erase AI from a writer’s research work these days is to give up digital tools altogether, given that even the search engines we have been using for 20 plus years are now largely powered by large language models.</p>
        <p>In that case, the best way to think about the research tools in <em>The Faith Received</em>, including the chat bot feature, is that it is roughly equivalent to using an LLM for search–which if you use Google you are already doing. The only difference is that the TFR chat bot has an extremely defined data set that makes it more limited in most ways, but far more powerful in some particularly valuable ways for our readers.</p>
        <p>Second, again as we said last week, research is one thing, but the work of making arguments and structuring sentences and paragraphs is another. The latter is essentially human work. So the point here is not that you can outsource your theological formation to a machine. Rather, you can use the research tools provided in TFR to help deepen your own theological knowledge and understanding so that you can then turn around and convey the truths you have learned in your own words to others, either in writing or in conversation.</p>
        <p>In any case, our testing has found that all text generated by the TFR chat bot registers as AI generated when tested in Pangram. So if someone submitted a piece to us that was “written” by our own chat bot, we would be able to recognize it very easily both because Nadya and I are getting much better at recognizing LLM copy and because our testing tools would tell us. Anyone else who has concerns along these lines can also use the same tools we can to root out the use of LLM-generated copy in text purported to be human written.</p>
        <h3>But what about AI translations?</h3>
        <p>I can anticipate a skeptical reader replying here by saying that if the work of making arguments and structuring essays is human work, then surely so is the work of translation. I respect the argument and it is the one I have wrestled with the most as we have worked on this project. That said, there are a few factors that led me to think this is still a worthwhile endeavor.</p>
        <p>First point: The good enjoyed by the translator of a text and the good enjoyed by the reader of a translated text are distinct things. (While one could make a similar argument about the work of a writer and the work of a reader more generally, I do think it matters that we are talking here about translation, not writing more generally. Text wholly generated from AI is distinct from human-written text that is then translated into another language with the aid of AI.) The benefit I have taken from reading Bavinck in English, for example, is thanks to the particular work done by John Bolt and others who have translated the Dutch master. But there is nothing about my experience as a person reading Bavinck in translation that is uniquely enriched by the fact that John Bolt translated it rather than someone else. The pleasure he felt while translating is different from the pleasure I felt while reading in English. Both are significant, of course, but they are distinct. As a reader, my experience is that I am encountering Bavinck, via the mediation of Bolt. I am grateful for Dr Bolt’s labors. But the core person in my encounter here is Bavinck, not Bolt. That is the whole point of translation of such works, I think: for the translator to remain in the background and the words of the original author to be foregrounded. AI translations do not compromise that basic encounter, I think: When I read <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=amandus-polanus">Polanus</a> in English via <em>The Faith Received</em> I am encountering his words.</p>
        <p>Incidentally, I think many of us have had experiences of relating to works in a language we do not know in a relatively pragmatic way. 30 years ago I could have, theoretically, set out to use other available translation tools, such as they were, to translate Bavinck’s Dutch into English. Indeed, when I was attempting to learn German this is more or less what I did with certain texts we read in class: I got a German-English dictionary as well as my German textbook, and got to work translating. I did not know German at all; I simply had a couple reference books and was doing the best I could. There were two goods I was pursuing in that work. One was to learn German. The other was simply to render a text I could not read into language that I could through the use of various tools in the absence of a thorough knowledge of the original language.</p>
        <p>Any graduate student has had experiences along these lines at times. They do not need to speak French or German fluently; they simply need enough to allow them to understand the text they’re reading, and they relied upon various supplemental aids and tools to help them “unlock” the text. This project fully honors the second of those goods, I think, while relativizing the first in ways admittedly far more extreme than a dual-language dictionary does, but in ways not altogether different.</p>
        <p>Second point: One of the arguments Stiven made as he and I discussed this was that both of us could recognize how dramatically certain debates in reformed evangelicalism were changed by the translation of Bavinck’s Reformed Dogmatics. Well, he said, surely there are other previously untranslated texts that could prove similarly helpful and generative if only they were available to the average English speaker? I found that persuasive.</p>
        <p>Even thinking about my own denominational context in the Presbyterian Church in America, I am struck by the fact that the discourse in the PCA often amounts to southern presbyterians behaving as if southern presbyterianism is the only faithful expression of the reformed faith while missional PCA members act as if the tradition is somewhat of a liability in the work of evangelization and therefore must be relativized at certain points. What Bavinck did and what we hope TFR will do is show that both sides are wrong: Theological traditions are far broader than many realize and a closer encounter with the Christian intellectual tradition should make us better, more faithful evangelists, preachers, and theologians. Our theological traditions are treasures, not millstones.</p>
        <p>Third point: As a fourth-generation Nebraskan and first-generation college student, I have a strong draw toward egalitarian, democratized approaches to knowledge and learning. When <a href="/the-faith-received/author/?a=William%20Tyndale">Tyndale</a> was translating the Bible into English, a skeptical Catholic rebuked him, arguing that the common person was not equipped to understand scripture. Tyndale was undaunted and said that if he had his way a common plowboy would know the Bible better than most of the Roman church’s bishops. I have a similar inclination here. This library will not suddenly push college and seminary professors out of a job. It will simply put the texts that a small number of people have been able to read into the hands of a far larger group of people, so that they can read and learn from these texts for themselves. If anything, I hope this will make for better students who can now read primary sources for themselves, rather than relying on secondary sources in order to understand their respective theological traditions.</p>
        <p>Fourth point: Because we do care deeply about the integrity of our work and never want to betray the trust of our readers, we have tried to approach this project in the most transparent way possible. You can read our transparency commitment <a href="/the-faith-received/transparency/">here</a>. Additionally, the reason we are launching this in Beta today is so that prior to the official launch we can have a few months with readers interacting with the texts and the research tools and offering feedback.</p>
        <p>I will leave off there for now. But I do expect we will continue to have conversations about the use of AI in intellectual work in the months to come. We have an essay coming from me later this week on the site that offers a more extensive treatment of the question. The upshot, however, is straightforward: We can recognize some uses of AI are obviously wrong, some are obviously OK, and others are complex and require a certain degree of patience and tolerance for one another as we work through the issues together. We can’t promise we’ll always arrive at positions you agree with. But we will do our best to show our work, be honest about what we are and are not doing, and be open to constructive criticism about these issues.</p>
